### Q16. What is Jenkins, and why do we use it in DevOps?

Spoken answer:

"Jenkins is an open-source automation server that is mainly used for Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery.

In a real-time project, whenever a developer pushes code to GitHub, Jenkins can automatically detect the changes and start the pipeline.

The pipeline can perform different activities such as downloading source code, compiling the application, running unit tests, performing code quality checks, building Docker images, and deploying the application.

So, instead of performing all these activities manually, Jenkins automates the entire process and reduces human errors."

### Q17. Explain a real-time Jenkins CI/CD pipeline.

Spoken answer:

"In my project, we use Jenkins to automate the application build and deployment process.

First, developers push their code to GitHub.

Jenkins detects the code changes through a webhook and starts the pipeline.

The first stage is checkout, where Jenkins downloads the latest source code.

Then we perform the build using tools like Maven or Gradle, depending on the project.

After that, we execute unit tests and code quality analysis using SonarQube.

If all the checks pass, we build a Docker image and push it to a container registry.

Finally, we deploy the application to the target environment, such as an EC2 instance or Kubernetes cluster.

At each stage, Jenkins provides logs and build status, so we can easily identify failures."

